:: Meal Welcome Home [activity meal] {"position":"1975,2225"}
/*
{
        id: "welcome_home",
        passage: "Meal Welcome Home",
        isActive: false,
        mealType: ["dinner"],
        isImportant: true,
        dayType: ["weekday", "weekend"],
        cooldown: 0,
        once: true,
        lastSeen: -1,
        present: ["mom", "sister"],
        requirements: [
        ],
        set: [
            () => setup.getMealById("welcome_home").isActive = false,
        ]
}
   Activated by: "Meet Sister" (main_meetSister). My first dinner back home.
*/
$mom.callName has gone overboard. There's a lasagna the size of a suitcase in the middle of the table, garlic bread, a salad nobody is going to touch, and the good plates with the thin gold rim that normally only come out at Thanksgiving. Somebody lit a candle. Judging by the way $sister.callName keeps smirking at it, that somebody wasn't her.

/* IMAGE: images/meals/important/welcome_home.jpg | The kitchen table set for three with the good plates, a huge lasagna, a single candle. Mom beaming, Sister smirking at the candle. */

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $mom, text: "Sit, sit, you must be starving after all that driving. I made your lasagna, the one with the extra layer, and don't you dare tell me you stopped for burgers on the way." },
{ who: $player, text: "I had a granola bar somewhere around noon. Does that count?" },
{ who: $mom, text: "It absolutely does not count." },
{ who: $sister, text: "She also cleaned the bathroom twice today, just so you know. When I came back from two weeks of summer camp I got a frozen pizza and a reminder to get my laundry out of the car." },
{ who: $mom, text: "You were sixteen and you came home with a tattoo you thought I wouldn't notice, so I'd say the pizza was generous." },
{ who: $sister, text: "It's a very small tattoo. It's tasteful, mostly." },
{ text: "$mom.callName piles a slab of lasagna onto my plate that could feed a family of four, then adds a second piece of garlic bread before I can stop her." },
{ who: $mom, text: "And the car made it all right? $sister.callName said you were worried it wouldn't survive the highway." },
{ who: $player, text: "It survived. It started making a couple of new noises around the state line that I've decided not to think about, but it got me here." },
{ who: $sister, text: "It's just happy to be home, like its owner." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

For a while it's easy. $sister.callName tells us about a guy in her study group who brought a laminated schedule to the first meeting and has been personally offended ever since that nobody follows it, $mom.callName laughs until she has to put her fork down, and I eat more than I have in a month. My room upstairs is still full of boxes, but down here nothing has moved an inch: same chairs, same squeaky table leg, same magnet on the fridge from the vacation when I was nine.

Then $mom.callName refills my glass, and I can see the question coming before she asks it.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $mom, text: "So how's the company going, honey? And I mean really, not the version you tell people on the phone." },
{ text: "$sister.callName gets very interested in her salad. She already got my answer earlier, on the couch, and she didn't believe it either." },
{ who: $player, text: "It's... fine. It's in a slow phase, lots of building and not a lot of selling, which is apparently normal at the start. At least that's what every podcast says." },
{ who: $mom, text: "Well, you know you can stay here as long as you need, there's no rush at all. Your room is still your room, I didn't touch a thing." },
{ who: $sister, text: "She really didn't. The dragon poster is still up." },
{ who: $player, text: "Good. That dragon is a classic." },
{ who: $sister, text: "That dragon has an eight-pack and a sword the size of a canoe. You were twelve, I get it." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

$mom.callName reaches across the table and squeezes my hand, quickly, the way she did when I was a kid with a test the next morning. She means it kindly, and it still lands like a pat on the head.

I squeeze back and tell her the lasagna is the best thing I've eaten all year, which is true, and let the conversation drift back to the guy with the laminated schedule. Neither of them asks what I've been doing with all the boxes of parts upstairs, and I don't offer. I couldn't really explain it yet, and I'm a little surprised by how much I like having something they don't know about.

<<UpdateStat "+" "MomRelation" 2>>
<<UpdateStat "+" "SisterRelation" 2>>
